  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening Country Leadership by Bridging Accountability to Affected People (AAP) Knowledge Gaps to Achieve a Systemic Collective Approach</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Accountability to affected populations (AAP) has been part of the humanitarian reform agenda in the last three decades. AAP is one of the four mandatory responsibilities of the Humanitarian Country Teams (HCT) outlined in the HCT terms of reference endorsed by the Inter-Agency Standing Committee principals and referenced in the Leadership in Humanitarian Action Handbook for Resident Coordinators. This project focuses on strengthening leadership in HCTs by deepening humanitarian leaders understanding of the value of AAP and the structural barriers to collective approach, to increase the likelihood that leaders become advocates of AAP in humanitarian response and support planning to ensure improved AAP advocacy at all levels. To this end, IOM is facilitating the development of learning modules in collaboration with key partners to support the objective of improving leaders&apos; understanding of AAP and their own roles in ensuring affected populations are empowered to influence humanitarian assistance in planning and delivery.</narrative>
  </description>
